WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.550
- Thank you very much Priya and Ty,

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:12.402
for inviting me to this
very interesting workshop.

00:00:12.402 --> 00:00:15.570
And I might not be the
most obvious contributor

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:17.490
to workshop on inference.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.050
So, I'm a physicist by training,

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:22.650
but I've spent last
decade assessing science

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:26.430
and communicating science
as in translating physics

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:29.013
to physicists and to other scientists.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.160
And this talk is not about causation,

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.090
so it's quite a change of gear
from what you heard earlier.

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:37.380
It's also a shorter talk.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.470
So, I hope like we can
discuss a bit at the end.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.930
So, I'll talk about the scientific method

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.390
and the scientific discourse.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.720
And I'd like to start with the four, like,

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.030
maybe five paradigms of science

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:53.370
you might or might not have heard about.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:57.362
They were introduced by a
computer scientist, Jim Gray,

00:00:57.362 --> 00:00:59.400
who's also got a Turing award.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.580
And in 2007 he gave a talk

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:07.510
where he said like, there are three

00:01:08.451 --> 00:01:09.660
and now four paradigms of science.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:13.320
And these are, have
appeared in historical order

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:14.820
like empirical.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.593
That's how science first was,

00:01:18.502 --> 00:01:20.130
a description of natural observations

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:22.560
and description of natural phenomena.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.590
And in the next paradigm is theoretical,

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:29.103
where we starting using
models and generalizations.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.670
The third paradigm is computational

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.870
with the rises of computers

00:01:36.061 --> 00:01:40.470
where scientists have started
to simulate complex phenomena.

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.380
And what this is quite obvious,

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:45.778
but what Jim Gray said,

00:01:45.778 --> 00:01:49.380
"The fourth paradigm of
science is data exploration,

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.040
which brings together theory,
experiment, and simulation

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.380
and is a different way

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:58.410
of doing different scientific method."

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.593
And he talked about it in, as I said,

00:02:02.703 --> 00:02:05.160
he gave a talk in 2007

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:08.190
and months later he disappeared at sea.

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:11.070
So, he never lived to see, actually,

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:12.960
that he was quite right.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.110
And we've seen quite a lot of development,

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:19.980
and actually we see a
fifth paradigm emerging,

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.020
and I'll talk about that.

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:25.020
But like despite Jim Gray
not being able to explore

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:26.280
his ideas further,

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:30.120
his collaborators put together
a book based on his ideas

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.907
and contributed essays that is called

00:02:32.907 --> 00:02:33.840
"The Fourth Paradigm of Science"

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.640
and was publish in 2009

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:38.370
and is freely available on the internet.

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:43.080
So, what does this have to
do with inference at all?

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:47.490
Well, as we've gone through historically

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:50.331
through these different
paradigms of science,

00:02:50.331 --> 00:02:52.050
which actually all coexist today,

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:55.710
also the way inference works has changed.

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:57.870
Like, there's no one-to-one correspondence

00:02:57.870 --> 00:02:59.310
of paradigms of inference.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:00.660
I'm not claiming that.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:04.800
We can say that we started
with induction, deduction,

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:07.920
and we moved towards
statistical types of inference

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.653
in the age of computational
and data exploration.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:13.950
And what I claim is that now

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:16.260
in the fifth paradigm of science

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:18.990
we see things like
simulation based inference.

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:20.583
I talk a bit more about that.

00:03:21.422 --> 00:03:23.520
I'm no expert in
simulation based inference,

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.890
so don't grim me on that,

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:28.260
but we'll discuss it in a moment.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:32.190
So, I'll go a bit now into each paradigm

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:35.400
and show you how the
scientific discourse look like

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:37.170
in each of these paradigms.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:40.200
So, back to the basics
is the first paradigm

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:43.140
of direct observations
of natural phenomena.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:45.090
We can see the scientific discourse,

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:48.123
and I hear I'm taking a bit narrow view

00:03:48.123 --> 00:03:52.050
that the scientific discourse
is mainly scientific paper,

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:55.170
but it's true in more broadly.

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:57.060
So, here's an example.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:01.530
Edmund Halley in 1714
had seen a solar eclipse,

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:05.523
describes it in a paper
for the royal society.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:09.480
Here, in the second
paradigm start building

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:12.753
theoretical models to explain
empirical observations.

00:04:13.613 --> 00:04:17.880
And we have equations that
are solved analytically.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:22.320
And an example of that is
Maxwell's equations 1864.

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:25.980
This is the paper where he describes them.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:29.040
So again, we don't see a lot of change

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.470
in the scientific discourse so far,

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:34.693
but it's clear like science,

00:04:34.693 --> 00:04:37.588
scientific method itself is evolving.

00:04:37.588 --> 00:04:40.110
In the third paradigm, we have computers,

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:43.769
and we can solve complex
mathematical models

00:04:43.769 --> 00:04:44.602
by numerical simulations.

00:04:44.602 --> 00:04:48.690
So, and this is a nice example,
a paper I particularly like.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:51.180
This is the Metropolis Algorithm,

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.400
and it's interesting for several reasons.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:58.400
So, it's arguably the earliest
computational physics paper

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:00.450
ever published.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:05.340
And it describes what is
known as Metropolis Algorithm,

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:08.370
but actually Metropolis
himself didn't contribute

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:09.540
much to this paper.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:12.540
More, he was the one who provided time

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.400
on the computer on MANIAC.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.390
The people who actually
did the work were Arianna

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:23.390
and Marshall Rosenbluth helped
by Augusta and Edgar Teller.

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:26.310
And what tells us, let's again,

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:28.230
if you just look at the paper,

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:33.210
it's actually quite modern
despite being from 1953.

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:35.670
But, what we already notice here,

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:37.860
there are more contributors to this paper,

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:39.933
and they all have done different things.

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:43.860
And this will become even more so

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:46.140
in the fourth paradigm of science

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:48.540
where discovery is
enabled by the exploration

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:50.190
of massive data sets.

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:52.950
And the example I chose
here is the discovery

00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:54.780
of the Higgs boson.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:57.345
So, why is it interesting is

00:05:57.345 --> 00:06:00.900
this was like a great
achievement not only technically

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:02.820
from an experimental point of view,

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:05.430
but also from the data
analysis point of view.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:09.840
And already by 2012 had been used,

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:12.150
they had used lots of
machine learning to do

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:13.470
the data analysis.

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:17.073
And here like already
we're talking about 1000s

00:06:17.073 --> 00:06:20.730
of authors involved in in this paper.

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:22.080
So Jim Gray thought

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:27.060
that this data exploration
was considerably distinct

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:30.780
from the previous computational
paradigm to deserve

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:35.780
its own, you know, separate naming.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:36.990
How about the fifth?

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:39.630
Well this remains to be defined,

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:42.003
but there are lots of
ideas floating around.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:46.660
So, for example, this is from 2019

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:50.220
maybe computers will decide objectively,

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:53.550
which experiments yield the
biggest gain in our knowledge,

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:58.170
and they would drive the
experimental investigations.

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:00.196
Other ideas say that maybe

00:07:00.196 --> 00:07:03.330
computers will be robust simulators

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:06.840
to replace traditional
numerical simulation methods.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:10.260
This is a stance taken
by Christopher Bishop

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:12.270
who's at Microsoft Research.

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:14.280
And actually say like

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:17.220
the fourth paradigm of
science was published

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:18.390
by Microsoft Research,

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:21.439
so they like this idea very much.

00:07:21.439 --> 00:07:26.439
And then, we wrote a bit
more dramatic departure

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:28.260
from these ideas.

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:30.840
We wrote last year in an editorial

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:34.200
that maybe machines will
no longer be just tools,

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:35.610
but there'll be equal partners

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:37.800
in the scientific exploration,

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:40.470
and they will gain some
scientific understanding

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:42.690
that we share with human peers.

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:44.100
And that's a bit more radical,

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:47.373
but still like all
these ideas are kindred.

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:53.390
And now, I mentioned
simulation based inference,

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:56.550
and this is also expected to have

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:58.347
a profound impact on science.

00:07:58.347 --> 00:08:01.350
And it's actually related to the idea

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:03.210
of robust stimulators,

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:08.210
because this is how
they get the likelihood

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:10.260
in simulation-based inference.

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:13.770
So, all these ideas have
something in common,

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:16.470
AI will play a very important role.

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:18.690
How? We don't know yet.

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:21.330
And how will the
scientific discourse change

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:23.725
in this fifth paradigm?

00:08:23.725 --> 00:08:25.230
Again, we don't know.

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:28.740
But, has the scientific
discourse changed at all?

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:30.030
Well I say, "no,"

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:33.180
'cause we are still stuck in
the first three paradigms.

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:35.310
And again, I want to make it clear

00:08:35.310 --> 00:08:36.990
that by scientific discourse

00:08:36.990 --> 00:08:40.980
I take the narrower view
of scientific writing

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:44.190
as a way to convey ideas and observations

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:48.060
in a persuasive way to
your scientific peers.

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:50.060
Yeah, and this is what I understand here

00:08:51.155 --> 00:08:51.988
by scientific discourse.

00:08:51.988 --> 00:08:53.520
And why hasn't it changed at all?

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:56.190
So, you see like methodology has evolved.

00:08:56.190 --> 00:08:59.550
We use different
methodologies at this stage,

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:01.260
but the discourse hasn't.

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:02.880
Is it the format

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:04.740
or is the audience?

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:07.020
And I will explore this too,

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:09.000
and I'll give reason why it is

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:10.893
and a reason why it shouldn't be so.

00:09:12.210 --> 00:09:15.962
So, we're actually stuck in
print in terms of format.

00:09:15.962 --> 00:09:19.050
And this is should not
be surprising actually,

00:09:19.050 --> 00:09:23.550
because in the long history
of scientific publishing

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:26.790
online has been here
for a very short time.

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:30.630
So, the first journals
appear in 1665 and actually

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:33.480
the philosophical transactions
are still published today.

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:39.060
Like journals like "Nature"
and "Science" came much later.

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:41.550
And first online journals actually came

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:45.213
in the late '80s and
beginning of the '90s.

00:09:46.596 --> 00:09:47.940
And the preprint started.

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:51.150
The archives started around the same time.

00:09:51.150 --> 00:09:53.940
And so, these are like
new kids on the block

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:56.520
in the history of scientific publishing.

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:00.420
And "Nature" digitized
its full archive in 2008.

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:03.780
And as of 2007, I don't
know if they finished it,

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:06.240
but "Royal Society" was still digitizing

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:09.870
its full archive back to 1665.

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:14.610
So, we don't have
everything on online yet.

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:18.439
And purely online only
journals have only come

00:10:18.439 --> 00:10:20.910
very, very recently.

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:22.830
But, even for online only journals,

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:24.930
they are still stuck in print,

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:27.484
even though they've never been printed.

00:10:27.484 --> 00:10:32.310
Think of the PDF format that
any online journal still has.

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:36.000
Think of the table of contents
that signifies an issue.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:37.830
And if you are in the trade

00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:40.230
of publishing a journal like I am,

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:44.233
you'll encounter a lot very
frustrating restrictions

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:47.880
that have like historical
things like about

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:49.890
how you lay out things,

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:52.341
how you actually physically print

00:10:52.341 --> 00:10:53.880
and physically bind an issue,

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:56.970
which of course have no sense in this age,

00:10:56.970 --> 00:10:58.500
but they're still there.

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:00.715
And for like some 10 years now,

00:11:00.715 --> 00:11:03.783
people have been talking about
the article of the future.

00:11:05.838 --> 00:11:07.890
A lots of thought has been put into this,

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:10.093
and there've been experiments

00:11:10.093 --> 00:11:12.270
but in the end, like we still have

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:13.893
the same format as before.

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:17.190
But, I think we do need to change,

00:11:17.190 --> 00:11:20.233
because the scientific
narrative these days is

00:11:20.233 --> 00:11:22.140
a very complex one.

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:24.000
And it is very hard to distill

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:27.180
in the traditional print-bound format.

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:30.570
Like today, a scientific
article is not like

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:34.320
in a Halley's example,
like I've seen an eclipse,

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:36.780
and I'll write down what I've seen.

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:39.060
Increasingly relies on lots of code

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:42.840
and lots of data, maybe
huge amounts of data

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:45.060
that have gone through rounds and rounds

00:11:45.060 --> 00:11:47.610
of processing, of instruments

00:11:47.610 --> 00:11:49.290
that are complicated in themselves.

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:50.880
And the parameters they run on,

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:52.770
they're very important to describe.

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:55.800
The methods and the protocols,
we need to know about that.

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:59.310
And of course, the main story also relies

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:01.380
on a large corpus of knowledge

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:03.480
that is found in the bibliography.

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:06.450
And we see that these
bibliographies go longer and longer

00:12:06.450 --> 00:12:08.580
and longer as time goes.

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:12.450
So, everything has, all these
elements have to come together

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:17.280
and be distilled in a what is
essentially a print format.

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:19.530
Obviously, that's not possible.

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:20.943
How about the audiences?

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:26.130
Well, different disciplines
have very different cultures

00:12:26.130 --> 00:12:28.863
with their own jargon
and their own custom.

00:12:30.067 --> 00:12:32.610
And it's fair to say
without offending anyone

00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:34.920
that some disciplines are
more modern than others.

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:37.768
And you see some
disciplines are interesting

00:12:37.768 --> 00:12:40.680
in evolving and changing things,

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:42.540
also because they deal maybe

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:45.360
with more non-traditional products

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:48.600
like data or code, while others are not.

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:50.550
And what we've seen,

00:12:50.550 --> 00:12:54.270
there's a lot of resistance
in accepting to change

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:57.900
the scientific discourse by
exploring new article formats

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:00.510
and experimenting with
different narratives.

00:13:00.510 --> 00:13:01.980
This has to do with tradition

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:06.540
and also the fact that we tend to give

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:09.450
to associate rigor and authority

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:11.760
with traditionally established formats,

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:13.770
because if we try to present

00:13:13.770 --> 00:13:15.750
the scientific discourse in a new form,

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:20.460
it will simply not be seen as
authoritative and trustworthy.

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:23.070
But, at the same time, who's reading this?

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:26.610
Who's benefiting from
the scientific discourse?

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:30.030
And I really, really
like this infographic.

00:13:30.030 --> 00:13:33.030
Mind you, this is almost 10 years old.

00:13:33.030 --> 00:13:35.370
So, what it shows is
the mountain of paper.

00:13:35.370 --> 00:13:38.850
So, if you were to print the first page

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:42.570
of every single item that
is indexed in web of science

00:13:42.570 --> 00:13:46.110
and stuck it up in 10 years ago,

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:50.280
you would have got a pile as
high as Mount Kilimanjaro.

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:53.850
I'm curious how high this
pile has gone up now,

00:13:53.850 --> 00:13:56.198
but like my colleagues at "Nature,"

00:13:56.198 --> 00:13:58.350
they promised that they
will redo this infographic,

00:13:58.350 --> 00:14:01.080
so hopefully we'll see that next year.

00:14:01.080 --> 00:14:03.090
But, of this huge, huge pile,

00:14:03.090 --> 00:14:07.920
only the very, very, very
tiny top is what, okay,

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:10.140
if you consider citations as a proxy

00:14:10.140 --> 00:14:13.020
of how much the paper was read,

00:14:13.020 --> 00:14:14.463
maybe it's not a good proxy,

00:14:15.403 --> 00:14:18.270
but how much this paper is known,

00:14:18.270 --> 00:14:21.990
it still gives a very sad view

00:14:21.990 --> 00:14:23.940
of like how much is actually read

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:26.610
of this huge, huge mountain of knowledge.

00:14:26.610 --> 00:14:28.741
And it's true now

00:14:28.741 --> 00:14:31.170
that the scientific literature
is increasingly specialized.

00:14:31.170 --> 00:14:33.750
Like, even for people
from the same discipline,

00:14:33.750 --> 00:14:35.640
it's really hard to understand

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:37.560
what is going on let alone like

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:40.560
if you're trying to read
from other disciplines.

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:42.750
So, we are getting narrower and narrower,

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:45.030
and we understand less and less,

00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:46.950
and we have less and less time

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:50.280
of reading this whole
knowledge that is out there.

00:14:50.280 --> 00:14:53.430
And this literature we are producing,

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:55.470
this scientific discourse
we're producing is

00:14:55.470 --> 00:14:59.133
increasingly read by machines
rather than human beings.

00:15:01.290 --> 00:15:04.113
So, what point am I trying to make?

00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:09.900
Scientific method has evolved,
has become more complex,

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:12.940
and we are moving towards a new paradigm

00:15:13.792 --> 00:15:17.610
where AI in whatever form it
will play an important role.

00:15:17.610 --> 00:15:20.880
But, the scientific discourse
itself has not evolved

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:23.043
beyond the first three paradigms.

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:27.390
And I think that it's very important now

00:15:27.390 --> 00:15:28.500
that as we move

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:33.210
towards the fifth paradigm
to try to find new ways,

00:15:33.210 --> 00:15:35.270
new forms of scientific writing,

00:15:35.270 --> 00:15:39.150
scientific discourse that
are more representative

00:15:39.150 --> 00:15:41.130
of the scientific method,

00:15:41.130 --> 00:15:43.620
the complex narratives
we're trying to convey

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:45.787
and actually the audiences.

00:15:45.787 --> 00:15:48.333
'Cause, who we are we writing for?

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:52.950
And I think we need to adapt
the scientific discourse

00:15:52.950 --> 00:15:56.280
to the needs of the
21st century scientists.

00:15:56.280 --> 00:15:58.290
And I also think,

00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:03.290
and I experienced this a lot,
trying to publish new art,

00:16:03.510 --> 00:16:06.528
new forms of narratives,
I got a lot of pushback.

00:16:06.528 --> 00:16:11.528
So, I want to say like
let's be open-minded.

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:15.120
The scientific discourse is not fixed,

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:18.267
like the scientific methods
is not fixed, it's evolving.

00:16:18.267 --> 00:16:21.123
So should the scientific discourse.

00:16:21.990 --> 00:16:26.990
Now, before I end, I want to
be my own devil's advocate

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:30.390
and preempt the question
that you might have,

00:16:30.390 --> 00:16:33.150
and say, "Okay, like if nobody's reading,

00:16:33.150 --> 00:16:34.860
only like machines are reading,

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:37.827
why should we care about the
scientific discourse at all?"

00:16:39.113 --> 00:16:39.946
Yeah.

00:16:39.946 --> 00:16:41.500
And actually, I think

00:16:42.383 --> 00:16:44.340
the scientific discourse
will remain important

00:16:44.340 --> 00:16:47.580
as long as we human beings
are interested in science,

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:50.490
because this is how we share the science,

00:16:50.490 --> 00:16:52.050
we share the new ideas,

00:16:52.050 --> 00:16:54.990
and we share the
understanding we have gained.

00:16:54.990 --> 00:16:57.450
And a year ago we published

00:16:57.450 --> 00:17:00.607
this very interesting perspective called

00:17:00.607 --> 00:17:04.080
"On Scientific Understanding
with Artificial Intelligence."

00:17:04.080 --> 00:17:06.450
It was a bit of a strange type of review.

00:17:06.450 --> 00:17:09.630
So, the authors came up,
used like a definition

00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:13.500
of scientific understanding
from the philosophy of science,

00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:15.390
maybe not or people agree with that,

00:17:15.390 --> 00:17:19.620
but they used this definition
as an umbrella term to collect

00:17:19.620 --> 00:17:24.620
scattered literature about how
computers can help us achieve

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:27.360
understanding, scientific understanding.

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:30.810
And they use that to make a
survey of what has been done.

00:17:30.810 --> 00:17:33.310
And then, they finished
like with the discussion

00:17:34.283 --> 00:17:35.116
where they said like,

00:17:35.116 --> 00:17:38.670
"We propose a test for
scientific understanding,

00:17:38.670 --> 00:17:40.530
which is basically whether

00:17:40.530 --> 00:17:43.980
a human teacher or a
computer teacher is better

00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:47.820
at explaining to a student
a scientific concept.

00:17:47.820 --> 00:17:49.110
And you see where this comes.

00:17:49.110 --> 00:17:50.883
It's a sort of Turing test.

00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:55.463
And fine, I mean you
know, you might agree,

00:17:55.463 --> 00:17:56.617
you might disagree.

00:17:56.617 --> 00:18:00.124
But, in the same issue we had
a very different perspective

00:18:00.124 --> 00:18:02.947
from Matthew Schwartz who said like,

00:18:02.947 --> 00:18:07.947
"Actually AI is evolving
on a much faster time scale

00:18:09.559 --> 00:18:10.590
than human intelligence.

00:18:10.590 --> 00:18:14.523
So, very quickly AI will understand things

00:18:14.523 --> 00:18:17.100
that and will not be able
to explain them to us.

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:21.600
And that's fine, we should just
leave it to do the science,

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:24.240
and we'll go fishing."

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:25.890
Whether you're happy with that or not,

00:18:25.890 --> 00:18:27.960
again, it's debatable,

00:18:27.960 --> 00:18:32.490
but I think these different
perspectives highlight

00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:35.610
what I was trying to say
in the beginning is that

00:18:35.610 --> 00:18:38.790
no matter whether computers could

00:18:38.790 --> 00:18:42.870
or should take over in
doing science in what form,

00:18:42.870 --> 00:18:47.870
I think it's important that
they would explain that to us,

00:18:48.870 --> 00:18:50.940
'cause that's what we do in
science in the beginning,

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:52.110
in the first place.

00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:57.110
And how computers or AI will
develop a scientific discourse,

00:18:58.470 --> 00:18:59.580
again, we don't know,

00:18:59.580 --> 00:19:02.880
but I do hope that it's not the same

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:06.810
350 years old scientific article.

00:19:06.810 --> 00:19:09.725
So yeah, that's what I had to say.

00:19:09.725 --> 00:19:11.209
And thank you for your attention.

00:19:11.209 --> 00:19:14.209
(audience applauds)

00:19:17.250 --> 00:19:18.721
- Thanks so much Iulia.

00:19:18.721 --> 00:19:20.820
This reminds me of the conversation

00:19:20.820 --> 00:19:22.710
we were having just a couple of days ago

00:19:22.710 --> 00:19:24.870
at a colloquium at FDS.

00:19:24.870 --> 00:19:26.820
What is gonna count as a discovery

00:19:26.820 --> 00:19:31.080
if we are going to have machine learning

00:19:31.080 --> 00:19:33.130
sort of, you know, comb through our data,

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:36.360
get trained on the current
data and simulations?

00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:39.090
Are we, I mean the nature
of what we counts today

00:19:39.090 --> 00:19:41.040
as discovery is gonna
change fundamentally,

00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:44.160
so it's very interesting, that,

00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:45.960
but I mean do you have a suggestion

00:19:45.960 --> 00:19:48.360
of new forms of communication?

00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:50.850
Surely, you don't think TikTok videos are

00:19:50.850 --> 00:19:52.170
the way we should go, right?

00:19:52.170 --> 00:19:54.330
- No, definitely, definitely not.

00:19:54.330 --> 00:19:59.330
But, I think this very
workshop is a nice example of,

00:20:00.690 --> 00:20:02.880
you know, how we communicate differently

00:20:02.880 --> 00:20:04.380
in different fields

00:20:04.380 --> 00:20:08.220
and what we consider to
be a discovery, again.

00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:11.490
Because, for me it was very
interesting the different ideas

00:20:11.490 --> 00:20:14.820
and philosophy of science
and which one is correct.

00:20:14.820 --> 00:20:17.100
I mean they're very beautiful arguments

00:20:17.100 --> 00:20:18.213
for this and for that,

00:20:19.224 --> 00:20:21.360
but like how do you
decide like in your field,

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:24.840
you know, ultimately it's
the experimental validation

00:20:24.840 --> 00:20:26.550
of the observations we have.

00:20:26.550 --> 00:20:29.370
So, for a lot of physics, right,

00:20:29.370 --> 00:20:31.410
theory can be as beautiful as it is.

00:20:31.410 --> 00:20:34.800
If it's not confirmed by
experiment, we don't care.

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:37.470
And in other fields, like
you mentioned medical,

00:20:37.470 --> 00:20:39.550
like it doesn't matter what you find

00:20:40.389 --> 00:20:42.720
if it's not going to
help me treat a patient.

00:20:42.720 --> 00:20:45.462
And in engineering, it doesn't matter

00:20:45.462 --> 00:20:48.783
if a device doesn't run
faster or consumes less.

00:20:49.714 --> 00:20:52.020
So I think the first question to ask is

00:20:52.020 --> 00:20:53.760
what do we want from science?

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:55.110
And in different branches

00:20:55.110 --> 00:20:57.869
of science we want different things,

00:20:57.869 --> 00:20:59.940
or we have different cost
functions, let's say.

00:20:59.940 --> 00:21:02.100
And from that cost function,

00:21:02.100 --> 00:21:04.200
we will define what the discovery is,

00:21:04.200 --> 00:21:07.290
and from that, the type of narrative

00:21:07.290 --> 00:21:10.953
that we want to use for
a scientific discourse.

00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:14.640
- Thank you very much.
Any other questions?

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:17.223
Eduardo, Imad and then Sean.

00:21:23.220 --> 00:21:24.990
- [Eduardo] Thank you.

00:21:24.990 --> 00:21:26.010
I was hoping you can comment,

00:21:26.010 --> 00:21:27.990
I'm not quite following 

00:21:27.990 --> 00:21:31.380
why you feel like the discourse
hasn't really changed.

00:21:31.380 --> 00:21:35.670
So, I have my view is that publication

00:21:35.670 --> 00:21:37.860
of science has changed profoundly,

00:21:37.860 --> 00:21:39.960
and I will be very curious to see

00:21:39.960 --> 00:21:42.000
a timeline along the paradigms

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:44.190
of how it's become from something

00:21:44.190 --> 00:21:46.090
that professional societies were doing

00:21:47.011 --> 00:21:48.197
to something that is today

00:21:48.197 --> 00:21:50.460
multi-billion dollar corporate business.

00:21:50.460 --> 00:21:53.688
So, part of that change has been,

00:21:53.688 --> 00:21:56.850
and another major change
in discourse is that

00:21:56.850 --> 00:22:00.762
it used to be the case that
you are describing science

00:22:00.762 --> 00:22:05.762
as conveying new ideas and
observations in a persuasive

00:22:06.930 --> 00:22:10.050
and rigorous way to his scientific peers.

00:22:10.050 --> 00:22:12.420
Ideas being maybe the
introduction and the discussion

00:22:12.420 --> 00:22:14.730
and the observations being the results.

00:22:14.730 --> 00:22:16.230
A major change has been

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:20.430
that in some of these outlets
there are no methods anymore,

00:22:20.430 --> 00:22:22.500
which seem to be, in my opinion,

00:22:22.500 --> 00:22:25.804
I mean you really have to
dig deep and deep and deep.

00:22:25.804 --> 00:22:26.700
I mean, it has changed.

00:22:26.700 --> 00:22:29.970
It was easy to read the
methods 20 years ago

00:22:29.970 --> 00:22:31.530
before the results.

00:22:31.530 --> 00:22:33.510
Now, I cannot if I get the paper,

00:22:33.510 --> 00:22:35.313
a copy of "Nature" in my hands.

00:22:36.243 --> 00:22:38.640
So, I'm not following
that, it hasn't changed.

00:22:38.640 --> 00:22:40.867
- So, there are two, two things.

00:22:40.867 --> 00:22:41.700
So, first thing you mentioned is

00:22:41.700 --> 00:22:44.326
the business model has changed.

00:22:44.326 --> 00:22:45.553
I agree with that.

00:22:45.553 --> 00:22:48.150
It's become from like
scientists like sending letters

00:22:48.150 --> 00:22:51.570
to an editor for
scientific society to share

00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:53.640
that knowledge with the peers

00:22:53.640 --> 00:22:56.940
to, as you say, a business model

00:22:56.940 --> 00:22:59.304
where people are publishing

00:22:59.304 --> 00:23:01.230
and companies are enabled
in this publishing.

00:23:01.230 --> 00:23:03.180
Yeah, I'm not arguing that.

00:23:03.180 --> 00:23:05.940
It's the scale of it
has changed dramatically

00:23:05.940 --> 00:23:08.250
and so does the economics of it.

00:23:08.250 --> 00:23:12.450
But like, if you look at
the scientific paper itself

00:23:12.450 --> 00:23:16.230
of how it looked 50
years ago, 100 years ago

00:23:16.230 --> 00:23:21.180
and how it looks now, at the in its first

00:23:21.180 --> 00:23:23.820
in this form, it's very much unchanged.

00:23:23.820 --> 00:23:26.430
I can read the paper from 100 years ago

00:23:26.430 --> 00:23:28.320
and I can understand it.

00:23:28.320 --> 00:23:30.360
I can read the paper now,

00:23:30.360 --> 00:23:32.310
it will have the same sections.

00:23:32.310 --> 00:23:34.989
It will have the same
presentation of results.

00:23:34.989 --> 00:23:37.800
It will have, yes maybe like a lot

00:23:37.800 --> 00:23:40.110
of the complexity will be sent

00:23:40.110 --> 00:23:43.770
into supplementary information
or in a method section.

00:23:43.770 --> 00:23:45.930
- [Eduardo] But, it's the
defining feature of what we do.

00:23:45.930 --> 00:23:46.890
That is science.

00:23:46.890 --> 00:23:48.541
It's the methods.

00:23:48.541 --> 00:23:52.140
Otherwise, it's a interesting
story in the Sunday newspaper.

00:23:52.140 --> 00:23:53.100
- Well yes, of course,

00:23:53.100 --> 00:23:56.013
but what I'm saying is the, you know,

00:23:56.970 --> 00:23:58.410
- [Host] Structurally,
you are saying right?

00:23:58.410 --> 00:23:59.967
- Structurally, the way structurally

00:23:59.967 --> 00:24:02.310
and format wise, it has not changed.

00:24:02.310 --> 00:24:03.390
It is the same way.

00:24:03.390 --> 00:24:05.775
You're not sending a paper

00:24:05.775 --> 00:24:08.730
that is like 1,000 pages long

00:24:08.730 --> 00:24:11.520
that contains all the
descriptions of the entire,

00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:15.183
if you look like a, you know,
at Chadwick's experiment,

00:24:15.183 --> 00:24:19.530
you can find everything he
did in 20 pages or 50 pages.

00:24:19.530 --> 00:24:21.030
And now, of course you can't do that,

00:24:21.030 --> 00:24:23.100
because we are relying on more

00:24:23.100 --> 00:24:25.440
and more information
that is hidden, right?

00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:28.410
It's in either in
supplementary information.

00:24:28.410 --> 00:24:30.630
It's sending to other references,

00:24:30.630 --> 00:24:33.846
but the pure format of how you present,

00:24:33.846 --> 00:24:34.679
you have the abstract.

00:24:34.679 --> 00:24:36.810
You have the introduction of the problem.

00:24:36.810 --> 00:24:38.760
You have like the main results,

00:24:38.760 --> 00:24:40.770
and that structure has not changed at all.

00:24:40.770 --> 00:24:44.040
It's the same that it was
like 50 and 100 years ago.

00:24:44.040 --> 00:24:45.270
That's what I'm saying.

00:24:45.270 --> 00:24:47.520
And I feel that is restrictive,

00:24:47.520 --> 00:24:51.300
and because, and that
comes from the print format

00:24:51.300 --> 00:24:53.490
that originally existed.

00:24:53.490 --> 00:24:57.270
And I think that as you said,
everything should be there,

00:24:57.270 --> 00:24:58.890
everything should be accessible

00:24:58.890 --> 00:25:00.990
and should be able to dig

00:25:00.990 --> 00:25:04.502
in deeper and deeper and
deeper within the same.

00:25:04.502 --> 00:25:05.943
- [Host] Including wrong turns, right?

00:25:05.943 --> 00:25:06.989
- Pardon?

00:25:06.989 --> 00:25:08.248
- [Host] Including wrong turns

00:25:08.248 --> 00:25:09.314
that were made in the experiment.

00:25:09.314 --> 00:25:10.477
- That is another big issue,

00:25:10.477 --> 00:25:12.090
'cause we are only
reporting what it works.

00:25:12.090 --> 00:25:13.363
- Yeah.

00:25:13.363 --> 00:25:14.370
- We are never reporting
what it doesn't work,

00:25:14.370 --> 00:25:16.020
which is equally important.

00:25:16.020 --> 00:25:18.890
We're also selectively reporting, right?

00:25:18.890 --> 00:25:22.473
We are reporting like things that,

00:25:23.670 --> 00:25:26.793
and the stories we tell are obviously not.

00:25:27.750 --> 00:25:29.130
So, what I'm trying to say,

00:25:29.130 --> 00:25:33.210
the stories we tell are very much shaped

00:25:33.210 --> 00:25:36.340
by the collective experience of scientists

00:25:37.448 --> 00:25:40.860
and the science is not in vacuum, right?

00:25:40.860 --> 00:25:44.580
It's a very complex social
and economical human activity.

00:25:44.580 --> 00:25:46.140
And what we are reporting

00:25:46.140 --> 00:25:49.320
in this scientific discourse
is very much shaped

00:25:49.320 --> 00:25:54.320
by the cultural, economical
and historical context, right?

00:25:55.409 --> 00:25:58.950
So, when you write up a discovery,

00:25:58.950 --> 00:26:01.924
you write it differently
depending, you know,

00:26:01.924 --> 00:26:03.090
what you want to say.

00:26:03.090 --> 00:26:05.400
Sometimes, you know, it's not objective

00:26:05.400 --> 00:26:06.690
how you write a paper.

00:26:06.690 --> 00:26:10.140
It's very much what story
you're trying to get across.

00:26:10.140 --> 00:26:12.900
And that story depends
on the funding you got,

00:26:12.900 --> 00:26:14.910
on the journal you publish in,

00:26:14.910 --> 00:26:16.620
on what your peers think,

00:26:16.620 --> 00:26:18.588
and so on and so on.

00:26:18.588 --> 00:26:19.421
It's right?

00:26:19.421 --> 00:26:21.000
And the way we do this,

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:23.310
like we have all different
ways of doing this

00:26:23.310 --> 00:26:24.750
in different outlets,

00:26:24.750 --> 00:26:28.320
in different fields and so on.

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:33.320
So, but overall, the structure
has remained the same.

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:36.213
Although the complexity is
much, much, much bigger,

00:26:37.050 --> 00:26:39.810
the structure and the format
has remained the same.

00:26:39.810 --> 00:26:40.643
- Imad.

00:26:41.970 --> 00:26:43.560
- [Imad] Sorry, I don't have a microphone.

00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:45.618
- Oh, move that.

00:26:45.618 --> 00:26:49.539
- [Imad] Will it reach? Great.

00:26:49.539 --> 00:26:51.900
Okay, couple of questions.

00:26:51.900 --> 00:26:55.920
So, the first is in terms of like thinking

00:26:55.920 --> 00:26:57.480
beyond like the PDF,

00:26:57.480 --> 00:27:01.263
or thinking beyond that
sort of print-like format,

00:27:02.250 --> 00:27:05.400
you know, are you looking
at some of the things

00:27:05.400 --> 00:27:07.760
that are being marketed now?

00:27:07.760 --> 00:27:08.860
I know like MIST tools

00:27:09.865 --> 00:27:11.280
and like sort of integrated code,

00:27:11.280 --> 00:27:15.210
interactive figures in a
sort of web-based framework.

00:27:15.210 --> 00:27:18.093
These are things that people
are trying to come up with

00:27:18.093 --> 00:27:20.193
that try to make that jump.

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:24.390
And I guess my question is A, you know,

00:27:24.390 --> 00:27:27.270
there's one problem of longevity.

00:27:27.270 --> 00:27:30.360
So, a PDF is a quite longevous thing.

00:27:30.360 --> 00:27:33.124
You know, we think that
standard will be around a while,

00:27:33.124 --> 00:27:33.990
and you can always print it out.

00:27:33.990 --> 00:27:37.170
If you create a beautiful
web-based journal

00:27:37.170 --> 00:27:39.090
that has all of these interactive features

00:27:39.090 --> 00:27:41.370
and can access the data underneath

00:27:41.370 --> 00:27:44.427
and can do all of this
cool stuff, you know,

00:27:44.427 --> 00:27:46.530
what happens if that website shuts down

00:27:46.530 --> 00:27:47.550
or something like that?

00:27:47.550 --> 00:27:48.660
- [Host] Crashes, right? Yeah.

00:27:48.660 --> 00:27:49.493
- [Imad] Yeah. And-

00:27:49.493 --> 00:27:50.326
- [Host] Data gets lost.

00:27:50.326 --> 00:27:54.071
- [Imad] Or yeah, or, even just
from an economic standpoint,

00:27:54.071 --> 00:27:55.050
you know, that company shuts down.

00:27:55.050 --> 00:27:58.440
The host of that server is no
longer available, et cetera.

00:27:58.440 --> 00:28:01.740
And then also, portability.

00:28:01.740 --> 00:28:03.420
You know, you can download a PDF,

00:28:03.420 --> 00:28:04.470
and take it around with you

00:28:04.470 --> 00:28:07.560
and read it on your phone
or on the train or wherever.

00:28:07.560 --> 00:28:09.840
So, I'm just wondering, you know, how much

00:28:09.840 --> 00:28:13.890
of the lack of perceived willingness

00:28:13.890 --> 00:28:17.631
in the field to embrace something

00:28:17.631 --> 00:28:19.080
that is better attuned to handling

00:28:19.080 --> 00:28:21.600
the amount of data and complexity is due

00:28:21.600 --> 00:28:25.140
to some of these just extremely
practical kind of things?

00:28:25.140 --> 00:28:27.690
And what do we do to address that?

00:28:27.690 --> 00:28:29.280
- No, you're absolutely right.

00:28:29.280 --> 00:28:32.940
There's a lot of that involved.

00:28:32.940 --> 00:28:36.450
Ultimately, the primary role

00:28:36.450 --> 00:28:40.560
of the scientific journal
is to archive knowledge.

00:28:40.560 --> 00:28:42.810
If that knowledge cannot be archived

00:28:42.810 --> 00:28:46.170
and read 100, 200 years from now,

00:28:46.170 --> 00:28:47.970
then it's not very useful.

00:28:47.970 --> 00:28:52.200
And print for the good or
the bad, like it is readable.

00:28:52.200 --> 00:28:57.200
And indeed, like the PDF,
we read Halley's paper,

00:28:57.330 --> 00:28:59.520
we read Newton's papers today.

00:28:59.520 --> 00:29:01.140
And you're absolutely right,

00:29:01.140 --> 00:29:04.563
lot of digital products are ephemeral,

00:29:05.580 --> 00:29:08.010
and that is a problem indeed.

00:29:08.010 --> 00:29:10.860
And there are lots of
people who working on

00:29:10.860 --> 00:29:12.900
how to archive science,

00:29:12.900 --> 00:29:16.710
and how to keep this knowledge

00:29:16.710 --> 00:29:20.100
and how to port basically these formats

00:29:20.100 --> 00:29:22.710
to the next devices and the next devices.

00:29:22.710 --> 00:29:25.740
And that itself is like
a really big problem

00:29:25.740 --> 00:29:28.050
that librarians are looking into,

00:29:28.050 --> 00:29:30.390
the publishers are looking into.

00:29:30.390 --> 00:29:34.323
And indeed it is difficult to solve,

00:29:34.323 --> 00:29:39.323
but it's very much related to how we,

00:29:41.400 --> 00:29:43.000
more like, to how we do science

00:29:43.842 --> 00:29:45.648
and how we preserve what we've,

00:29:45.648 --> 00:29:47.918
all the data that we have,
all the code that we have,

00:29:47.918 --> 00:29:52.230
and also relates a lot to
reproducibility, right, science.

00:29:52.230 --> 00:29:54.270
And it is very difficult question.

00:29:54.270 --> 00:29:55.890
It's not, and you're right,

00:29:55.890 --> 00:29:58.980
maybe one of the reasons
in contributing factors

00:29:58.980 --> 00:30:01.440
why the transition is very slow,

00:30:01.440 --> 00:30:06.440
it's because it takes a while
to catch up with these things.

00:30:07.860 --> 00:30:09.900
And there are lots, as you said,

00:30:09.900 --> 00:30:13.920
there are lots of tools people
have developed, which are,

00:30:13.920 --> 00:30:15.990
you know, again, very ephemeral,

00:30:15.990 --> 00:30:17.940
because if the creators leave,

00:30:17.940 --> 00:30:19.440
or like there's no more funding,

00:30:19.440 --> 00:30:21.780
those tools are not available anymore.

00:30:21.780 --> 00:30:26.370
And they're like big institutions,
like for example, CERN.

00:30:26.370 --> 00:30:29.310
They play a big role in
the archival of data,

00:30:29.310 --> 00:30:31.740
in establishing good practices.

00:30:31.740 --> 00:30:34.560
And I think this can be
done, but you are right.

00:30:34.560 --> 00:30:38.940
It's one of the reasons
why things have been slow.

00:30:38.940 --> 00:30:40.457
(audience applauds)

00:30:40.457 --> 00:30:41.820
- [Host] And let me thank Iulia

00:30:41.820 --> 00:30:43.270
for a great provocative talk.

